    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/hess-20-3843-2016",
    "abstract": "Abstract. This study explores whether climate models with higher spatial resolutions provide higher accuracy for precipitation simulations and/or different climate change signals. The outputs from two convection-permitting climate models (ALARO and CCLM) with a spatial resolution of 3\u20134 km are compared with those from the coarse-scale driving models or reanalysis data for simulating/projecting daily and sub-daily precipitation quantiles. Validation of historical design precipitation statistics derived from intensity\u2013duration\u2013frequency (IDF) curves shows a better match of the convection-permitting model results with the observations-based IDF statistics compared to the driving GCMs and reanalysis data. This is the case for simulation of local sub-daily precipitation extremes during the summer season, while the convection-permitting models do not appear to bring added value to simulation of daily precipitation extremes. Results moreover indicate that one has to be careful in assuming spatial-scale independency of climate change signals for the delta change downscaling method, as high-resolution models may show larger changes in extreme precipitation. These larger changes appear to be dependent on the timescale, since such intensification is not observed for daily timescales for both the ALARO and CCLM models.",
    "field": "Hydroclimatology",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the poor performance of coarse-resolution climate models in simulating local precipitation extremes and challenges the common downscaling assumption that climate change signals are independent of model resolution, which often fails to capture crucial convective processes.",
    "method": "This study compares high-resolution, convection-permitting climate models to their coarse-scale driving models by validating historical daily and sub-daily precipitation extremes against observations and by contrasting their projected climate change signals to assess the added value of higher spatial resolution.",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate whether high-resolution climate models provide more accurate simulations of local precipitation extremes and different climate change signals compared to their coarse-resolution driving models.",
